    "weeklyGoalMethodTitle": "Weekly Goal and Streak",
    "@weeklyGoalMethodTitle": {
        "description": "Method sheet title"
    },
    "weeklyGoalMethodSummary": "Your streak is the number of weeks in a row you met your weekly session goal. The week still under way never breaks it. The goal starts at 3 sessions a week and can be set from 1 to 7. The WHO and ACSM guidelines recommend muscle-strengthening work on at least 2 days a week. In habit research, missing a single opportunity did not materially set back forming the habit, and the habit kept strengthening for about two months.",
    "@weeklyGoalMethodSummary": {
        "description": "Weekly goal method sheet: summary"
    },
    "weeklyGoalMethodLimitations": "Habit studies follow groups over weeks to months; how long a habit takes varies widely between people. A streak measures consistency, not progress.",
    "@weeklyGoalMethodLimitations": {
        "description": "Weekly goal method sheet: limitations"
    },
    "weeklyGoalMethodOwnChoices": "The default of 3 sessions a week and the 1–7 range are Compound's own choices. Only the floor of 2 days a week comes from the guidelines.",
    "@weeklyGoalMethodOwnChoices": {
        "description": "Weekly goal method sheet: Compound's own choices"
    },
    "stepGoalMethodTitle": "Daily Step Goal",
    "@stepGoalMethodTitle": {
        "description": "Method sheet title"
    },
    "stepGoalMethodSummary": "Unless you choose your own goal, it is 8,000 steps a day, or 7,000 from age 60. In pooled cohort studies, the risk of dying early stopped falling at about 8,000–10,000 steps a day under 60, and at about 6,000–8,000 from 60. A later meta-analysis found most of the benefit by about 7,000 steps compared with 2,000.",
    "@stepGoalMethodSummary": {
        "description": "Step goal method sheet: summary"
    },
    "stepGoalMethodLimitations": "These are observational studies: people who walk more differ in other ways too, so the figures show association, not cause. Phones and watches count steps differently.",
    "@stepGoalMethodLimitations": {
        "description": "Step goal method sheet: limitations"
    },
    "stepGoalMethodOwnChoices": "The exact defaults of 8,000 and 7,000 are Compound's own picks within the ranges the studies found.",
    "@stepGoalMethodOwnChoices": {
        "description": "Step goal method sheet: Compound's own choices"
    },
    "adherenceBandMethodTitle": "On-Target Band",
    "@adherenceBandMethodTitle": {
        "description": "Method sheet title"
    },
    "adherenceBandMethodSummary": "A day's calories count as on target when they land within 10% either side of that day's target. Protein counts once you reach its target. US food labels may understate calories by up to 20% before a product counts as misbranded, so a tighter band would mostly measure label error rather than your eating.",
    "@adherenceBandMethodSummary": {
        "description": "On-target band method sheet: summary"
    },
    "adherenceBandMethodLimitations": "No study validates a particular band width. Logging errors and label errors both feed into the figure.",
    "@adherenceBandMethodLimitations": {
        "description": "On-target band method sheet: limitations"
    },
    "adherenceBandMethodOwnChoices": "The 10% band is Compound's own choice, sized to sit inside typical food-label error.",
    "@adherenceBandMethodOwnChoices": {
        "description": "On-target band method sheet: Compound's own choices"
    },
